Okay, I have zero interest in iOS or anything related
to it, but this claim seems unsubstantiated to me.

In particular:
# The Products transacted through the Service are licensed,
# not sold, to You for use only under the terms of this
# license, unless a Product is accompanied by a separate
# license agreement, in which case the terms of that
# separate license agreement will govern, subject to Your
# prior acceptance of that separate license agreement.

IANAL, but the way I read that, if Bob's NetHack Port for
iOS comes with a copy of the NetHack license, then the
terms of the NetHack license apply, just as you ought to
expect them to, and the remainder of the App Store user
agreement is irrelevant.  Unless I'm missing something
rather major, that means your objection is specious,
if not disingenuous.

I can understand not reading the entire agreement.  (It is
rather long.)  I can even understand not reading any of it
at all, if you simply don't care about it (e.g., if you
don't have any interest in iOS for other reasons).  This,
however, is the very first paragraph, and it seems rather
odd to make specific claims about the User Agreement (e.g.,
that it is incompatible with certain licenses) without
taking the very first paragraph into account.

> Like I said: buy an uncrippled device next time.

I do tend to agree with that.  The mere fact that you can't
run any software on the thing unless Apple approves of it
would be a problem for me.  I tend to want to run custom
software that I write myself for some things (e.g., I wrote
my own SRS in Perl), so I can't see paying money for a
device that won't let me do that.  YMMV.


